body:
@zcorpan Thanks for keeping an interest in this! Unfortunately I don't think that exposing the orientation would help much. What I was asking for was a way to disable automatic rotation by EXIF in some other way than using CSS. In my use case the images are preprocessed before they appear in the DOM. It is very impractical (and seems unintuitive) that I have to rely on CSS for operations that are JS-only. In my current workaround I have to add hidden `img` and `canvas` elements to the DOM just for the CSS property to be picked up properly before processing continues.

I was hoping for something like this:
```js
let img = new Image();
img.orientation = 'none'; // or 'from-image', maybe also 'portrait', 'landscape'
img.src = 'mysrc';
```
This way I could control the image orientation without having to use CSS. For cross-origin images without CORS, this would have to be ignored, too, of course.

Thinking about this a little more, `intrinsicOrientationAngle` and `intrinsicOrientationFlip` *could* be used to manually restore the original image, so it might address my need after all. It's still a workaround but better than having to use CSS:
```js
let img = new Image();
img.src = 'mysrc';

// wait for image

let canvas = document.createElement('canvas');
canvas.width = img.width;
canvas.height = img.height;
let ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');

ctx.translate(canvas.width / 2, canvas.height / 2);
ctx.rotate(-1 * img.intrinsicOrientationAngle * Math.PI / 180);
if (img.intrinsicOrientationFlip) {
   ctx.scale(-1, 1);
}
ctx.drawImage(image, -image.width / 2, -image.width / 2);
```

I would like to suggest that this problem could be solved via createImageBitmap.  CreateImageBitmap has an imageOrientation option.  Unfortunately that spec and the CSS images level 3 spec were written without knowledge of each other, so we are in this awkward situation where passing {imageOrientation: "none"} to createImageBitmap is equivalent to {image-orientation: from-image} in CSS.  createImageBitmap does not currently have an option that is equivalent to CSS's {image-orientation: none}, but it would be pretty easy to add that.

Before moving forward, I would like to add instrumentation to chromium to measure whether anyone is using {ImageOrientation: "none"} explicitly with createImageBitmap. If not, we could change the createImageBitmap spec to match the CSS semantics and change the default to "from-image", which would avoid breaking backwards compat for call sites that use the default.

Then, to draw an image to a canvas without using the EXIF orientation, one could simply do this:

```
let img = new Image();
img.src = 'mysrc.jpg';

img.onload = () => {
    createImageBitmap(img, {imageOrientation: "none"}).then((bitmap) => {
        ctx.drawImage(bitmap, 0, 0);
    });
}

```
